+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The OpenBSD command sandbox is currently unavailable without the legacy
execution promise. Restricted filesystem profiles are rejected before command
execution, including profiles with network access enabled explicitly.

The kernel clears unveil rules on execve unless execpromises are installed.
Using unveil alone in a launcher would therefore run the target with no
filesystem restrictions. The backend checks this limitation in both the
controller and helper and fails closed. It does not request exec or error.
Network isolation and managed-proxy containment are also unsupported.

The danger-full-access profile is an explicit opt-out and continues to run
commands without a sandbox. This package does not silently select it when a
restricted profile fails. getexecpath(3) is still used to resolve helper paths.
The internal OpenBsdPledgeUnveil identifier is retained for compatibility.

Managed/restricted sessions are rejected at session creation, before hooks,
MCP or shell snapshots run. Configuration-only commands such as login, doctor
and MCP administration can still load configuration. The app-server/daemon has
no --sandbox switch of its own; to start a session through it, set
sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access" in ~/.codex/config.toml or declare an
external sandbox. Do not use the daemon to obtain isolation it does not have.

The regression script reports unsupported sandbox execution with exit 77;
it must not be interpreted as a passing security test. --quick omits compiler
invocations when a compatible implementation is available in the future.

The installed executables carry the WXNEEDED flag, because V8 compiles with
W^X, so the filesystem holding ${LOCALBASE} must be mounted with the wxallowed
option. Check it with

	mount | grep wxallowed

and, if it is missing, add wxallowed to the /usr/local line in /etc/fstab.

You may use OpenAI commercial services or misc/llama.cpp, for the last
one you should add into ~/.codex/config.toml:

	model_provider = "local"
	model = "local"
	web_search = "disabled"

	[model_providers.local]
	name = "llama-server"
	base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1"
	wire_api = "responses"

Codex uses a lot of memory, it starts with 256Mb per agent/worker
thread, and you may need to bump your limits (`ulimit -d`) or raise
datasize limit in used login class..

Also, Codex may use a lot of open files, you may need to raise both
the system-wide maxfiles and used login class limits.

Git, Node.js (npm) and xdg-utils are installed as runtime dependencies. Git
is used by repository/worktree/apply operations; npm by explicitly configured
npm plugins; and xdg-open by browser login/OAuth. Plugins and marketplaces are
disabled by default; enable features.plugins=true to permit upstream
synchronization, installation or marketplace add/upgrade. Other MCP servers
and hooks may require separately packaged executables. Browser login prints
the URL if the launcher fails, and device-auth remains usable.
OpenBSD ksh is recognized as the POSIX shell and preferred over bash/zsh when
the user shell is not an explicit choice. Shell snapshots are limited to
bash/zsh; the ksh/sh path runs commands without a snapshot. Clipboard access
uses X11 when DISPLAY is set, and Secret Service needs a D-Bus session and
provider.

The local app-server daemon is enabled for eligible interactive sessions by
upstream's daemon_auto_start feature (enabled by default). It uses the same
executable as the CLI, installed at:

	${LOCALBASE}/libexec/codex/bin/codex

${LOCALBASE}/bin/codex is a link to that executable. The code-mode host is
installed beside it, and ${LOCALBASE}/libexec/codex/codex-package.json records
the package's version and native OpenBSD target. Codex checks the manifest and
executable versions before a new daemon start; no npm or Node.js is needed.

The daemon runs as the invoking user. Codex starts it on demand, detaches it
from the terminal, and leaves it running for other clients. Lifecycle commands:

	codex app-server daemon start
	codex app-server daemon version
	codex app-server daemon restart
	codex app-server daemon stop

PID records, logs, settings, and recovery data remain under CODEX_HOME
(default ~/.codex). The advertised socket is under app-server-control there;
on Unix it points to a protected socket under /tmp/codex-daemon-<uid>.
The daemon inherits the environment of the terminal that starts it, shared
by later clients. There is no system rc.d service and no restart after reboot.

The package manager owns the executable, host, and manifest. Use pkg_add -u
to update them, then `codex app-server daemon restart`. A running process keeps
its old image; an incompatible daemon version is reported with restart
instructions rather than being silently reused or internally replaced.
Restart also picks up port revisions with an unchanged upstream version.

Codex's daemon updater and --from-cli package replacement are disabled, not
the daemon. The /daemon update menu directs updates to pkg_add. No daemon
executable or package manifest is copied to ~/.codex/packages. Other optional
user-installed components and existing packages are left alone.

When migrating from an internally managed daemon, stop it with the old CLI
before replacing the package. No user-managed package files are deleted.
